<h2 id="categories-section-equalizers" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="0027">4.10</span> Equalizers</h2>

<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="0028">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0028">Definition <span data-tag="0028">4.10.1</span>.</a> Suppose that $X$, $Y$ are objects of a category $\mathcal{C}$ and that $a, b : X \to Y$ are morphisms. We say a morphism $e : Z \to X$ is an <em>equalizer</em> for the pair $(a, b)$ if $a \circ e = b \circ e$ and if $(Z, e)$ satisfies the following universal property: For every morphism $t : W \to X$ in $\mathcal{C}$ such that $a \circ t = b \circ t$ there exists a unique morphism $s : W \to Z$ such that $t = e \circ s$. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
As in the case of the fibre products above, equalizers when they exist are unique up to unique isomorphism. There is a straightforward generalization of this definition to the case where we have more than $2$ morphisms. 
</p>
